<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pamir Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pamirs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pamir Mountains are a high mountain system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, lying mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> and reaching into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, with peaks above 7,000 metres. Known as the 'Roof of the World', they were crossed by branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ancient Saka groups lived in and around them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains.jpg" alt="Pamir Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caroline Gutman · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gutman_Karakul_lake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Pamirs stand at a junction with several other great ranges: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a>, the Karakoram, the Kunlun, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>. Much of the range lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorno-badakhshan-autonomous-region">Gorno-Badakhshan</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>. To the south they border the Hindu Kush along <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>'s Wakhan Corridor, to the north they join the Tian Shan along the Alay Valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a>, and to the east they extend to the range that includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s Kongur Tagh. The highest peak of the core is Ismoil Somoni Peak, 7,495 metres, known from 1932 to 1962 as Stalin Peak and from 1962 to 1998 as Communism Peak.</p><p>About 12,500 square kilometres, roughly a tenth of the Pamirs, is glaciated, and the Vanch-Yakh Glacier, 77 kilometres long, is the longest glacier outside the polar regions. Winters are long and bitterly cold and summers short and cool. Annual precipitation of about 130 mm supports grassland but few trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The lapis lazuli found in Egyptian tombs is thought to come from the Pamir area of Badakhshan. About 138 BCE Zhang Qian reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Fergana Valley</a> northwest of the Pamirs, and Ptolemy vaguely describes a trade route through the area. From about 600 CE Buddhist pilgrims travelled on both sides of the Pamirs to reach <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and in 747 a Tang army was on the Wakhan River. In Old and Middle Chinese the range was called the Onion Range, after the wild onions growing there. The Northern Silk Road, about 2,600 kilometres long, connected the Chinese capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a> and crossed the Pamirs towards the west.</p><p>In 1838 Lieutenant John Wood reached the headwaters of the Pamir River. The British and Russians agreed in 1873 that the Afghan frontier would run along the Panj. Russian military-scientific expeditions mapped most of the Pamirs between 1871 and about 1893. In 1892 a Russian battalion under Mikhail Ionov camped near the present Murghab, and in 1893 the Russians built a fort there. The last blank areas around the Fedchenko Glacier were mapped in 1928 by the German-Soviet Alay-Pamir Expedition.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sheep herding in the upper meadows is the main source of income of the region, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> is mined in the west. The Pamir Highway, one of the highest international roads in the world, runs from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> to Osh in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a> and is the main supply route of the isolated region.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pamir Mountains” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Kushan king who had been refused a Han princess sent his viceroy Xie with 70,000 men across the <b>Pamirs</b> in 90 CE against the Han general Ban Chao, who cut off their supplies and forced them to withdraw, after which the Kushans sent tribute to the Han court.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…Saka Tigraxauda, 'with pointed caps', probably east of the Caspian and along the Syr Darya; the Saka Haumavarga, probably in Fergana and the <b>Pamirs</b>, whose name is usually linked to the ritual drink hauma; and the Saka Paradraya, 'beyond the sea', the Scythians north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Rivalry with Tang China, the Turks and the Arabs continued in the <b>Pamirs</b>, where the Tang general Gao Xianzhi's expedition of 747 seized Little Balur, a Tibetan ally.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The arrangement suggests a loose confederation in which a paramount ruler coexisted with powerful regional lords, each controlling towns, valleys and the routes through the <b>Pamirs</b> and Hindu Kush.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Their irrigation technology had first developed in the Bactria-Margiana Archaeological Complex west of the <b>Pamirs</b>, though how it reached the Tarim is unclear, and their staple crops, wheat and barley, also came from the west.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>Shule, the Chinese name for Kashgar, was a city-state at the foot of the <b>Pamirs</b>, recorded by Han sources from the 2nd century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-shule">Kingdom of Shule</a></li></ul></section>
